# The CPU slice of the live bot (task 4.14, D-124): ddnet-ai-bot.service runs in it (Slice=ddnetaibot.slice) and nothing else does.
# Why a slice of its own: cgroup v2 shares CPU only among SIBLINGS. The agents' builds, tests, arenas and training run in user.slice
# (weight 100), and a service in system.slice competes with them as part of system.slice (weight 100 as well), whatever CPUWeight= the service
# itself sets: in the measurements a weight on the service alone helped only partly and Nice= alone not at all (docs/research/cpu-priority.md). A top-level slice
# competes with user.slice and system.slice directly, so its weight is the share that counts.
# The name has NO dash on purpose: systemd reads "a-b.slice" as a child of "a.slice" (which would get the default weight 100 and be the one
# that competes at the top). deploy/install-launcher.sh installs this file with the bot unit; the test in crates/ddnet-ai/tests/deploy_units.rs
# keeps the two in step.
#
# CPUWeight= (systemd.resource-control(5): 1..10000, default 100, cgroup v2 cpu.weight): 1000 is ten times user.slice's share when the CPUs are
# contended and changes nothing on a quiet machine (the bot itself uses ~0.2 of a core). It is a weight, not a reservation or a limit, and not
# real-time scheduling: nothing here can starve the system, only make the agents' work wait for the bot's few milliseconds per decision.
# IOWeight= (cgroup v2 io.weight, same range): takes effect only with the BFQ or iocost I/O controller; this VPS's disk has scheduler `none`, so
# today it changes nothing. A no-cost default for a host that has one. (The measurements never set it.)

[Unit]
Description=CPU share of the DDNet-AI bot (task 4.14): the bot's CPU priority over the agents' work
Documentation=file:///home/ubuntu/aiddnet/DDNet-AI/deploy/README.md

[Slice]
CPUWeight=1000
IOWeight=1000
